#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Define the Hamiltonian {#step-2-define-the-hamiltonian}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Define the Ising Hamiltonian with the Qiskit `SparsePauliOp` class. To do "
"this, specify the angles `theta_x` $(\\theta_x)$, `theta_z` $(\\theta_z)$, "
"and `theta_zz` $(\\theta_{zz})$, which are related to the transverse field "
"strength (`g_x`), the longitudinal field strength (`g_z`), and the Ising "
"coupling strength (`J`), respectively. Specifically, "
"$\\theta_x=2g_x\\Delta{t}$, $\\theta_z=2g_z\\Delta{t}$, and "
"$\\theta_{zz}=2J\\Delta{t}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use the `SparsePauliOp.from_sparse_list` method to instantiate the "
"transverse field Hamiltonian (`Hx`), the longitudinal field Hamiltonian "
"(`Hz`), and the coupling Hamiltonian (`Hzz`). The "
"`SparsePauliOp.from_sparse_list` method takes as input a list of tuples. "
"Each tuple contains a Pauli string, the indices of the qubits that the Pauli"
" operators in the string act on, and the coefficient of the Pauli string. "
"For more details, see the "
"[`SparsePauliOp`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparsePauliOp)"
" API documentation. The qubit indices come from the `problem_nodes` and "
"`problem_edges` objects defined earlier. The full tilted-field Ising "
"Hamiltonian is the sum of these three Hamiltonians.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Construct the Trotterized quantum circuit {#step-3-construct-the-"
"trotterized-quantum-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, construct the quantum circuit that corresponds to the Trotterized "
"evolution of the qubits in `problem_nodes` under the tilted-field Ising "
"model. To simulate the time evolution, construct a sequence of quantum "
"circuits with an increasing number of Trotter steps (for example, 1, 2, and "
"3 steps). Set the number of Trotter steps, `num_trotter_steps`, to 10, and "
"create a range, `trotter_steps`, that contains the numbers from 1 to "
"`num_trotter_steps`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To construct the quantum circuit for a specific number of Trotter steps, use"
" the Qiskit "
"[`evolved_operator_ansatz`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.evolved_operator_ansatz)"
" function. This function takes as input the tilted-field Ising Hamiltonian, "
"defined earlier as a `SparsePauliOp` object, and outputs the quantum circuit"
" that corresponds to the evolution. Store the circuits in a dictionary, "
"`trotter_circ_dict`, whose keys are the numbers of Trotter steps and whose "
"values are the corresponding quantum circuits. The code inserts barriers "
"between Trotter steps only to make the circuit diagram easier to read. You "
"can remove the code that inserts the barriers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note that `evolved_operator_ansatz` outputs a parameterized quantum circuit "
"with a parameter `t` that takes the value of $\\Delta{t}$ in the evolution "
"operator. Because the definitions of the `theta` angles already include "
"$\\Delta{t}$, set all parameters to `t=1`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/trotterized-time-"
"evolution-ltm/extracted-outputs/8c0f5da2-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The exponentials of the Pauli operators $X$, $Z$, and ${Z}{Z}$ are the following rotation gates:\n"
"$$\n"
"R_x(\\theta_x)=e^{-i{\\frac{\\theta_x}{2}}X}\n"
"\\quad;\\quad\n"
"R_z(\\theta_z)=e^{-i{\\frac{\\theta_z}{2}}Z}\n"
"\\quad;\\quad\n"
"R_{zz}(\\theta_{zz})=e^{-i{\\frac{\\theta_{zz}}{2}}ZZ}\n"
"$$\n"
"So the Trotterized evolution becomes:\n"
"$$\n"
"e^{iHt} \\approx \\Bigg[\n"
"\\bigg(\\prod_{k\\in{N}}{R_x(\\theta_x)}\\bigg)\n"
"\\bigg(\\prod_{k\\in{N}}{R_z(\\theta_z)}\\bigg)\n"
"\\bigg(\\prod_{\\begin{matrix} {(k,l)}{\\in}{E} \\\\ {k<l} \\end{matrix}}{R_{zz}(\\theta_{zz})}\\bigg)\n"
"\\Bigg]^r\n"
"$$\n"
"The previous circuit diagram shows the repeated pattern of the circuit. Each step contains $R_x(\\theta_x=0.53)$ and $R_z(\\theta_z=0.1)$ gates on each qubit, and an $R_{zz}(\\theta_{zz}=1)$ gate between each pair of qubits where two-qubit interactions are allowed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Construct the observable for average magnetization "
"{#step-4-construct-the-observable-for-average-magnetization}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The observable for the average magnetization is as follows:\n"
"$$\n"
"H_M = \\frac{1}{|N|}\\Bigg(\\sum_{k\\in{N}}{Z_k}\\Bigg)\n"
"$$\n"
"where $|N|$ is the size of the set $N$. Use the `SparsePauliOp` class again to define the observable `Hm`, which corresponds to the average magnetization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 5: Compute the expectation value with the LTM AI-QEM function "
"{#step-5-compute-the-expectation-value-with-the-ltm-ai-qem-function}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute the expectation value of the average magnetization observable, `Hm`,"
" for each circuit in `trotter_circ_dict`. To do this, transpile each "
"circuit, apply the layout of the transpiled circuit to `Hm`, and create a "
"list of primitive unified blocs (PUBs). Each PUB contains the transpiled "
"circuit for a specific number of Trotter steps and the corresponding "
"observable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Then pass the list of PUBs to `ltm_function`, which you defined at the "
"beginning of the tutorial. You can either pass transpiled circuits with the "
"layout applied to the corresponding observables, as shown in the following "
"code, or pass the transpilation options through `options`. If you pass the "
"transpilation options, the function transpiles the circuits and applies the "
"layout to the observables.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compare against ZNE {#compare-against-zne}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute the average magnetization with the zero-noise extrapolation (ZNE) "
"method available in `qiskit-ibm-runtime`. Define the `EstimatorV2` primitive"
" to compute the expectation values, and enable ZNE error mitigation with "
"`estimator.options`, as shown in the following code.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sparse Pauli dynamics results {#sparse-pauli-dynamics-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses the classical sparse Pauli dynamics (SPD) algorithm [[2, "
"3]](#references) to evaluate the average magnetization. SPD was previously "
"applied to simulate the kicked transverse-field Ising model experiment in "
"[[4]](#references). This tutorial treats the SPD results as ideal, and "
"compares the results from the LTM AI-QEM function against them. To keep this"
" tutorial simple, it doesn't include the code that generates the SPD "
"results. Instead, the results are provided in the `spd_results` list. The "
"implementation of the SPD algorithm by its authors is available in "
"[[5]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compare the results {#compare-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To compare the mitigation methods, plot the results from LTM AI-QEM, ZNE, "
"and the unmitigated hardware execution against the SPD reference for each "
"number of Trotter steps. The code also prints a table of key performance "
"metrics for a quantitative comparison.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/trotterized-time-"
"evolution-ltm/extracted-outputs/84a5d8f2-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The LTM AI-QEM results closely follow the SPD results for all Trotter steps."
" LTM AI-QEM reduces the mean absolute error (MAE) to 0.055, compared with "
"0.249 for ZNE and 0.266 for the unmitigated results. This is a 79.2% "
"improvement over the unmitigated results, whereas ZNE provides a 6.4% "
"improvement. The corresponding root mean squared error (RMSE) values are "
"0.062 for LTM AI-QEM, 0.270 for ZNE, and 0.285 for the unmitigated results. "
"LTM AI-QEM achieves this improvement without additional QPU time, because "
"the correction is applied during post-processing.\n"
msgstr ""

